# NopsAI Product Wiki Source Map

This document is the repository-side source map for the in-app product wiki at
`services/ui/src/pages/ProductDocs.tsx` and its data model at
`services/ui/src/features/product-docs/model.ts`.

The wiki is intentionally grounded in the current repository implementation. It
should not describe future-state infrastructure, storage, autoscaling, or
cloud-specific automation unless that capability is backed by runtime code,
deployment files, tests, and focused docs.

The in-app wiki must be usable as a field reference. A user who sees
`llm_profile` under a step, `dashboard.mode` under an output item, or
`allowed_callers` under an external trigger should be able to search that exact
directive and learn what it does, where it can be used, what it does not grant,
and which options are currently supported.
GitOps drift documentation must state that drift review presents Git and Nopsai
panes with line numbers, synchronized scrolling, and highlighted removed Git
lines and added desired lines before push.
Runner-private-registry documentation must be searchable by
`docker_config_json`, `runner_registry_credentials`, `registry_credential_ref`,
`imagePullSecrets`, and `RegistryAuth`, and must clearly separate
infrastructure-owned platform pull secrets from NopsAI-owned runner credential
assignments. It must state that the legacy `/v1/internal/registry-auth/docker`
broker route is removed and that Docker runners use bootstrap-delivered
`NOPSAI_REGISTRY_DOCKER_CONFIG_B64` for local per-image RegistryAuth.
Runner routing documentation must be searchable by `dispatcher_routing`,
`effective_routing`, `runner_scopes`, Scope runner assignments, and Team runner
assignments. Runner lifecycle documentation must be searchable by runner
ejection. It must distinguish the GitOps-owned configured route map from the
live effective routing view derived from registered runner scopes and from
runtime-only registered runner deletion, route cleanup, and blocklisting.

The wiki must be self-contained. It may list repository files as implementation
evidence, but it must not depend on outbound GitHub, external documentation, or
other source links for the actual explanation. If a focused repo document has
important behavior, field, option, command, or example detail, copy the
maintained summary into the in-app wiki article and keep the file path only as
verification metadata.

## Source Priority

Use this order when updating product documentation:

1. Runtime code, schemas, and route behavior.
2. Docker Compose and Helm deployment files.
3. Focused markdown under `doc/`.
4. Runnable and copyable artifacts under `examples/`.
5. Root README examples.
6. UI help text only when it agrees with implementation.

## Documentation Model

The in-app wiki separates content purpose from reader audience. Do not use one
mixed category to describe both.

Content type values:

| Type | Use for |
| --- | --- |
| `tutorial` | End-to-end learning paths with prerequisites, steps, validation, cleanup, and next steps. |
| `how-to` | Task-oriented procedures for users who already understand the concept. |
| `concept` | Explanation of product ideas, boundaries, and architecture. |
| `reference` | Exact schema, API, CLI, configuration, and behavior facts. |
| `runbook` | Operational response procedures with symptoms, checks, resolution, rollback, and escalation. |
| `troubleshooting` | Diagnostic pages that lead the user from symptom to likely cause. |

Audience values:

| Audience | Reader |
| --- | --- |
| `new-user` | First-time product users and evaluators. |
| `automation-author` | People writing pipelines, triggers, schedules, approvals, outputs, and GitOps config. |
| `operator` | SRE, support, and operations users running or diagnosing the platform. |
| `administrator` | Platform owners configuring services, teams, access, runners, and releases. |
| `security` | Security, compliance, audit, and least-privilege reviewers. |
| `developer` | Integration and repository developers using NopsAI from code or Git workflows. |

Every procedural page should prefer structured prerequisites and procedure
steps over hiding instructions in prose. Every reference page should prefer
structured field rows with path, type, required/default behavior, scope,
constraints, permissions, security notes, and examples. Source documents and
runbooks should be structured objects, not plain strings, so the UI can render
purpose, access, checks, diagnostics, and escalation consistently. Source
evidence renders as non-clickable repository paths.

